ne, WITHOUT looking at the actual lemmas.\n\nThe concept is \"magnetization\" — the process of making something magnetic.\n\nLet me think about Arabic candidates:\n\n\nWorking from the definition, I'm considering roots related to magnetism. The word مغنط (magnet) comes from the root غ-ن-ط or م-غ-ن-ط, and I can derive verbal forms from this. Since I'm generating blindly without access to existing lemmas, I'm brainstorming candidates like مَغْنَطَ and related forms that would express the process of magnetization. I'm working through the morphological derivations more carefully now. For the quadriliteral root م-غ-ن-ط, Form II gives مَغْنَطَ with the verbal noun تَمَغْنيط, while Form V تَمَغْنَطَ produces تَمَغْنُط. I'm also considering compound phrases like جَذْب مغناطيسي and التأثير المغناطيسي as alternative ways to express magnetic concepts, though I need to verify the exact derivation from the original root. مغناطيس:\n- مَغْنَطَة: فَعْلَلَة pattern, verbal noun for magnetization process\n- تَمَغْنيط: تَفْعيل pattern (from Form II)\n- تَمَغْنُط: تَفَعْلُل pattern (verbal noun of Form V reflexive/ingressive)\n\nActually looking at this more carefully:\n- مَغْنَطَة is the pattern for the action/process noun (like زَلْزَلَة from زلزل)\n- تَمَغُّط is the Form V verbal noun\n- مَغْنَطِيَّة would be the \"magnetism\" (property), not the process\n\nNow I'm weighing the options for expressing the active process of magnetization. تَمْغِيط from Form II captures that sense of actively making something magnetic, while مَغْنَطَة works as the quadriliteral verbal noun. تَمَغْنُط leans more toward the reflexive meaning of becoming magnetized rather than the active process. I could also consider compound expressions using جَذَبَ (to attract) or شَدَّ (to pull) with magnetic modifiers, though these feel less direct than the derived forms. I'm working through the morphology of the quadriliteral root م-غ-ن-ط more carefully now. The Form I verbal noun مَغْنَطَة seems most natural for the basic action, while Form II تَمَغْنُط would express the reflexive sense of becoming magnetized. For \"magnetic induction\" specifically, the compound حَثٌّ مَغْنَاطِيسِيّ works as a descriptive phrase, though I'm still uncertain whether some of these derived forms align perfectly with standard Arabic morphological patterns. I'm working through different derivation patterns for magnetization in Arabic. Beyond the straightforward verbal noun forms, I could also consider descriptive constructions like \"magnetic transformation\" or \"acquisition of magnetism,\" though these feel less elegant. The most natural candidate remains the quadriliteral Form I verbal noun, which captures the active process of magnetizing most directly. I'm refocusing on the definition itself rather than trying to match existing lemmas. The Arabic definition emphasizes it's a process that makes material magnetic, either temporarily or permanently. The most straightforward derivation would be **مَغْنَطَة** as a standard verbal noun from the quadriliteral root. Looking at the quadriliteral verb forms, I can derive several related terms: مَغْنَطَة follows the standard pattern for the verbal noun of magnetization, while تَمَغْنُط represents the reflexive form describing the process of becoming magnetized. Beyond these morphological derivations, I could also express the concept through compound constructions like إِحْداث المَغْنَاطِيسِيَّة for \"inducing magnetism\" or use adjectival phrases like حَثٌّ مَغْنَاطِي.\n\nNow I'm moving to the database stage to verify which of these candidates actually exist as lemmas and how they align with the English equivalents of magnetization and magnetic induction.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":245,"cache_read_input_tokens":32720,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":245},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_availab