{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01KfxygsLiFCKrfNiMpbVciA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can see from the results:\n1. **مغنطة** is well attested in multiple Arabic dictionaries with translation \"magnetization\" — confirmed by multiple ARABTERM sources (Physics, Medical, Geology, Printing/Electricity)\n2. **تمغنط** appears in the geology dictionary in the combined entry \"مَغْنَطَة، تَمَغْنُط\" alongside مغنطة — as a combined entry suggesting both terms are used\n3. **حث مغناطيسي** / **حث مغنطيسي** = magnetic induction (confirmed, but this is a different concept from magnetization!)\n\n\n4. **تحريض مغنطيسي** is another term for magnetic induction found in geology sources\n\nI'm noticing that \"magnetic induction\" and \"magnetization\" are actually distinct physical concepts — induction refers to magnetic flux density while magnetization is the material's response — yet they're grouped together in the English synset. The existing lemmas I have are تمغنط and مغنطة, so now I need to search for Arabic full-text keywords and explore the root further.\n\nLooking at the existing lemmas more closely, تمغنط appears in phrases like \"شدة التمغنط\" and \"تيار تمغنط,\" while مغنطة is well documented. I'm checking whether these need diacritical marks added — تمغنط should be updated to تَمَغْنُط and مغنطة to مَغْنَطَة, which I've already found with proper diacritics in the Unified Medical Dictionary.\n\nNow I'm running a Maqayis query for the root and searching the Arabic full-text database.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7679,"cache_read_input_tokens":32965,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7679},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56968204-b17c-48e3-a4d4-868583b0