ough I'm reconsidering whether this is better classified as a lexical gap created by the introduction of modern science rather than a cultural omission.\n\nLooking at the existing definition now, I notice it uses colloquial phrasing that doesn't fit Al-Waseet's style, and the spelling of مغناطيسية needs updating to the modern standard مَغْنَطِيسِيَّة. I should revise it to use more formal language like تَكَسَّبُ بها or تصبح بها instead of تجعل.\n\nI've drafted a revised definition: \"عمليَّةٌ فيزيائيَّةٌ تَكَسَّبُ بِها مادَّةٌ ما الخاصِّيَّةَ المَغْنَطِيسِيَّةَ كسبًا دائمًا أو مؤقَّتًا\"\n\nNow I'm reconsidering حث مغناطيسي — I was too quick to dismiss it. In physics contexts, \"magnetic induction\" can actually describe the process of inducing magnetism, which overlaps with magnetization, and some textbooks do use these terms interchangeably for the process itself.\n\nBut looking at ARABTERM's entry for حث مغنطيسي, it maps to \"magnetic induction\" in the physics domain, where the distinction is clear: B represents magnetic flux density (also called magnetic induction), while M is the magnetization vector. Since the synset is specifically about magnetization, not flux density, removing حث مغناطيسي was the right call.\n\nNow I'm moving forward with the final YAML and deciding whether to keep the existing definition or refine it to match proper Arabic lexicographic conventions.\n\nI'm gathering example sentences for both مغنطة and تمغنط, then cross-referencing the diacritization against معجم اللغة العربية المعاصرة to ensure accuracy. I'm noting that both forms share the same borrowed root مغنط (a four-letter root) with no corresponding Maqayis entry since Ibn Faris wouldn't have documented borrowed Greek terms. Now I'm reviewing the detailed definition from the contemporary dictionary to ensure I capture the complete meaning of magnetization as both a process and a result. ة في مجال مغناطيسي. Now I'm considering the confidence score—both lemmas are well-established i